These domains are effective finite complete lattices. Weakening preserves
joins and meets.

[Exact observation semantics]\label{thm:adequacy}
At the least fixed point, a weak or strict observation bit is true
exactly when its corresponding finite recursive judgment is derivable.

[Decidability]\label{thm:decide}
Both $\succeq_\star$ and $\succ_\star$, and therefore $\cw,\cs$, are
decidable on all ordinary finite inputs.

[Substitution and contexts]\label{lem:structural-stability}
The pair on ordinary endpoints is preserved by ordinary type-preserving
substitution and by every original well-typed context.

[Ordinary template grafting]\label{lem:grafting}
If $A$ is an ordinary template and $\W p q$, then
$\W{A[x:=p]}{A[x:=q]}$, even when $p$ contains marks.

[Original reduction orientation]\label{thm:orient}
Every beta, $D0$, $DS$, $R0$, and $RS$ reduction, including its context
and substitution instances, strictly decreases under $\cs$.

[Arithmetic accessibility]\label{lem:arithmetic-accessibility}
For each fixed $S,\mathcal F$, the theory $\ID$ proves accessibility
of every well-typed auxiliary term under $R_S$.

[Well-foundedness and individual bound]\label{thm:upper}
The confined strict relation is well founded, and
$\rk_{\cs}(s)<\BH$ for every ordinary term $s$.

[Native carrier]\label{lem:carrier}
$W\cs U$, and $W\cs T$ implies $W\cs PT$ for every ordinary
closed $T:B$ carrying the stated support guard.

[Indexed carrier]\label{lem:indexed-carrier}
$W_i\cs U$, and $i\cs j$ implies both
$W_i\cs W_j$ and $W_i\cs PW_j$.

[Native finite-type iteration]\label{thm:iteration}
$\It_A$ realizes $(\alpha,f,x)\mapsto f^\alpha(x)$ with convention
\textup{(I)}, even when $f$ is not monotone.

[Exact descending height]\label{thm:height}
The descending height of $\cs$ is $\BH$, already cofinally witnessed
by closed native T terms of outer type $o\to o$.

[Finite inner normalization]\label{lem:inner-termination}
Every exact zero-class replacement strictly decreases
\(\mathcal M+\mathcal V\), and neither summand increases.

[Growth and modular decrement]\label{lem:control}
The functions \(f_*\) and \(f_b\) are even-valued primitive recursive
functions, majorize \(G\), and satisfy
\begin{equation}\label{eq:growth}
 f(n+1)\ge2^{2^{f(n)}},\qquad f(n)<G(n+1).
\end{equation}
They are eventually bounded by one fixed finite level of the hierarchy.
For every \(m\ge1\),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:potential}
 p(f(n))\equiv p(n)-1\pmod m
 \qquad(n\ge\max\{m,B_f\}).
\end{equation}

[Sparse endpoint comparison]\label{m:lem:endpoints}
For a fixed nonzero limit $\lambda$, $J_\lambda$ is strictly increasing.
Its distinct norm inputs have distinct terminal endpoints, ordered in the
same way. For any two distinct endpoints $Z<W$ one has
\begin{equation}\label{m:eq:sparse}
 W\ge2^{Z+1}.
\end{equation}
Every value with endpoint $Z$ lies strictly between $Z$ and $2Z$ and below
the next value of $f$.

[Snyatkov's arithmetic supplier]\label{m:thm:snyatkov}
The first-order theory $\operatorname{Th}(\N;0,1,+,<,f)$, with the exact
function in \eqref{m:eq:f}, is decidable.

[Sparse peaks]\label{u:lem:sparse}
For an infinite input of norm $t\ge2$, write $Z=f^L(t)=f(s)$ as in \eqref{u:display:1-3}.
Then $0<L\le s-1$, and
\begin{equation}
           Z<\psi(\alpha)=Z+L<2Z<f(s+1).             \label{u:display:1-6}
\end{equation}
Distinct peaks $f(s)$ and $f(u)$ with $u<s$ satisfy $f(u)\le f(s-1)$.

[Identity normal form]\label{u:thm:identity}
For $E,F\in\mathcal P_d$, the identity $E=F$ holds at every positive
valuation exactly when $\NF(E)=\NF(F)$. If the forms differ, one can
effectively specify a positive countervaluation of the form
\begin{equation}
           x_i=w_i(f(k)+1),\qquad w_i,k\in\NN.        \label{u:display:2-2}
\end{equation}
Consequently universal equality in $\mathcal P_d$ is decidable.

[Range cells and signed rows]\label{u:lem:cells}
The following assertions have effective tail bounds.
For a nonlinear positive expression $E$, let $Z_i$ be its displayed open
tag peaks, and $Y=\max_i Z_i=f(s)$. Then
\begin{equation}
              Y<E<f(s+1),\qquad E<h_\omega(Y).        \label{u:display:3-1}
\end{equation}
For a value $V\in\operatorname{range}(f)$ on that tail,
\begin{equation}
 V<E\ \Longleftrightarrow\ (\exists i\ Z_i\ge V),\qquad
 V>E\ \Longleftrightarrow\ (\forall i\ Z_i<V).       \label{u:display:3-2}
\end{equation}
After cancellation of identical tags in a signed row, its eventual
pointwise sign is the coefficient sign of its greatest remaining peak,
once those peaks have been ordered. If no tags remain, the row is affine.

[Exact block recursion]\label{m:lem:kernel-recursion}
One has
\[
 E^1_c=1,\quad X^1_c=f,\qquad
 E^2_c(t)=f(t)/2-\lceil c/2\rceil+1.
\]
Define
\begin{equation}\label{m:eq:V}
 V_j(t)=f^{\,f(t)/2+j+1}(t).
\end{equation}
Then $X^2_{c,j}=V_{j-\lceil c/2\rceil}$ for every fixed signed $j$ on
its positive-depth tail. For $d\ge2$, the unsigned identity
$X^d_c=X^{d-1}_c\circ X^d_{c+d}$ and the count identity
\eqref{m:eq:Erec} hold on a computable tail. The signed identity
\eqref{m:eq:Xrec} holds when $d\ge3$ or $j\ge0$:
\begin{align}
 X^d_{c,j}&=X^{d-1}_{c,j}\circ X^d_{c+d},\label{m:eq:Xrec}\\
 E^d_c&=E^{d-1}_c\circ X^d_{c+d}+E^d_{c+d}.\label{m:eq:Erec}
\end{align}

[Counter expansion and collision]\label{m:lem:counter}
On an effective tail,
\begin{equation}\label{m:eq:counter-expand}
 E^r_c(t)=\frac H2+1-\lceil c/2\rceil
                  +\sum_{h=3}^r E^h_{c+h}(A_h),
 \qquad \frac H2<E^r_c(t)<\frac{3H}{4}.
\end{equation}
Suppose counters of ranks $r\ge s\ge3$ have equal leaders. Then they
have the same prefix index $c$. If $r=s$ their whole values are equal;
if $r>s$ the smaller-rank input is the corresponding $A_s$, and
\begin{equation}\label{m:eq:collision}
 E^r_c(t)-E^s_c(A_s)=\sum_{h=s+1}^r E^h_{c+h}(A_h).
\end{equation}
Every counter introduced on the right has rank strictly greater than $s$.
For distinct leaders, counter order agrees with leader order. Equal
leaders are ordered by counter rank, with equality at equal rank.

[Exact prefix splitting]\label{u:lem:prefix}
Let $\kappa$ be a fixed Cantor prefix and let $\eta$ be a nonzero limit
whose leading monomial is at most the least monomial of $\kappa$;
the zero prefix is also allowed. In particular one may take
$\kappa=\pi k$ and $\operatorname{lead}(\eta)\le\pi$.
For $b\ge c=\norm(\kappa)$,
\begin{equation}
 M(\kappa+\eta,b)=\kappa+M(\eta,b-c).                \label{u:display:4-2}
\end{equation}
A descent above $\kappa$ reaches a state $\kappa+n$ with finite remainder
before passing below $\kappa$. During that segment the remainder's exact
norm control is
\begin{equation}
                   G_c(v)=f(c+v)-c\ge f(v)>v.         \label{u:display:4-3}
\end{equation}

[Ordered controls and counts]\label{u:lem:counts}
For increasing integer controls $g\le h$, both strictly exceeding their
input, let $L_g,L_h$ be the respective norm-maximal descent counts.
Then
\begin{equation}
 \alpha\le\beta,\quad\norm(\alpha)\le\norm(\beta)
       \quad\Longrightarrow\quad L_g(\alpha)\le L_h(\beta). \label{u:display:4-4}
\end{equation}

[Exact peak word]\label{u:lem:peakword}
For each fixed limit $\lambda$, one computes a finite word $W_\lambda$ of
principal kernels such that the peak of $h_\lambda(A)$ is exactly
\begin{equation}
             Z_{\lambda,A}=W_\lambda(\norm(\lambda)+A). \label{u:display:4-5}
\end{equation}
The omitted count is positive: $h_\lambda(A)=Z_{\lambda,A}+D_{\lambda,A}$.

[One-stage reserve consumer]\label{u:lem:stage}
Let $\rho<\pi m$ be a fixed nonzero limit, $m\ge1$, and begin an ordinary
descent at $A=\pi(k+m)+n$, of norm $X\ge x\ge\norm(\rho)$.
At its first cut $\pi k+n'$ with finite remainder,
\begin{equation}
        n'>J_\rho(x):=f^{D_\rho(x)}(x)+D_\rho(x),    \label{u:display:5-1}
\end{equation}
where $D_\rho(x)$ is the count from
$\rho+(x-\norm(\rho))$.

[Effective profile reserve]\label{u:lem:reserve}
From a lower profile $P$ below $L>\omega$ one computes a nonempty word
\begin{equation}
 F=h_{\rho_l}\circ\cdots\circ h_{\rho_1},\quad
 F(z)>P(z)\ (z\ge z_0),\quad
 R=\sum_j(\norm(\rho_j)+1),\quad
 H=\max_j\norm(\operatorname{lead}(\rho_j)),          \label{u:display:5-2}
\end{equation}
with every $\rho_j<L$. If $\pi\ge\omega$ is principal and at least every
displayed leading monomial, a descent from $\pi R+n$, $n\ge z\ge z_0$,
has terminal norm greater than $P(z)$. The same conclusion holds with any
additional permanent numerical mass $c$.

[Direct kernel growth]\label{u:lem:direct}
For fixed $L>\omega$ principal and fixed lower profile $P$, one computes
$T$ such that, for $t\ge T$ and every genuine mass $0\le c\le t-\norm(L)$,
\begin{equation}
                         \KK Lc(t)>P(t).             \label{u:display:5-3}
\end{equation}
Also, $\KK\pi c(t)\le h_\pi(t)$ on its genuine domain.

[Cross-mass strict-input separation]\label{u:thm:adjacent}
For a fixed principal $L>\omega$ and fixed lower profile $P$ with reserve
data \eqref{u:display:5-2}, put
\begin{equation}
              T=\max(4,R+5,z_0,\norm L,H).            \label{u:display:6-1}
\end{equation}
For $t\ge T$, $0\le c\le t+1-\norm L$, and
$0\le e\le t-\norm L$,
\begin{equation}
       \KK Lc(t+1)>P(\KK Le(t)+\EE Le(t)).            \label{u:display:6-2}
\end{equation}
There is no ordering requirement on $c,e$. More generally the same
conclusion holds with any $u>t$ and any mass genuine at $u$ on the left.

[Limit-exponent mass separation]\label{u:thm:limitmass}
Let $L=\omega^\beta$ with $\beta$ a nonzero limit. For a fixed lower
profile $P$ and data \eqref{u:display:5-2}, put
\begin{equation}
           T=\max(4,R+4,z_0,\norm L+1,H).            \label{u:display:6-5}
\end{equation}
For $t\ge T$ and $0\le c<e\le t-\norm L$,
\begin{equation}
                \KK Lc(t)>P(\KK Le(t)+\EE Le(t)).    \label{u:display:6-6}
\end{equation}
Consequently the lexicographically larger pair $(\text{input},-\text{mass})$
gives domination against every fixed lower profile, on an effective
genuine tail. Equal pairs give equal values.

[Exact successor cut and count cocycle]\label{u:lem:successor}
Let $\theta=\omega^\gamma\ge\omega$, $L=\theta\omega$, and
$w=\norm\theta$. On the common genuine domain $t\ge c+w+\norm L$,
\begin{align}
 \KK Lc(t)&=\KK\theta c(\KK L{c+w}(t)),              \label{u:eq:cut}\\
 \EE Lc(t)&=\EE L{c+w}(t)
        +\EE\theta c(\KK L{c+w}(t)).                \label{u:eq:cocycle}
\end{align}

[Small principal kernels]\label{u:lem:small}
On their genuine domains,
\begin{equation}
 \KK\omega c(t)=f(t),\quad\EE\omega c(t)=1,\qquad
 \EE{\omega^2}c(t)=f(t)/2-\lceil c/2\rceil+1.         \label{u:display:7-4}
\end{equation}
Thus $\omega^2$ kernels at the same input have exactly the mass classes
$\lceil c/2\rceil$, ordered in reverse. The control has even values.

[Strict mass order above degree two]\label{u:thm:mass}
For every fixed principal $L\ge\omega^3$, a computable $T_L$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 t\ge T_L,\quad0\le c<e\le t-\norm L
                 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad\KK Lc(t)>\KK Le(t). \label{u:display:7-5}
\end{equation}
For a nonzero limit exponent take $T_L=\max(7,\norm L+1)$.
For $L=\theta\omega$, $\theta\ge\omega^2$, $w=\norm\theta$, take
\begin{equation}
 T_L=\max(4,\norm L,2w,\max(8,w),S_\theta),          \label{u:display:7-6}
\end{equation}
where $S_\theta=0$ at $\theta=\omega^2$ and otherwise $S_\theta=T_\theta$.

[Ground weak/strict equivalence]\label{cor:ground-equivalence}
On all actual ground-output sources with ground free variables, universal
weak and strict comparison are polynomial-DAG many-one interreducible.

[Hierarchy growth]\label{lem:growth}
Every $F_j$ is strictly increasing and $F_j(n)>n$. For $n\ge1$,
$F_j(n)\ge2^n$. The values of $G$ are powers of two, and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:growth}
 G(n)\ge2^{n+100},\qquad G(n+1)\ge2^{G(n)+1}.
\end{equation}

[Uniform residue envelopes]\label{thm:envelope}
There is an effective function $m\mapsto r_m\in\{0,\ldots,m-1\}$ such
that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f(x)\equiv r_m\pmod m$ for $x\ge m$;
\item for $s\ge1$, $j\ge2$ and $m\le2^{2^s}$,
      $F_j(s)\equiv r_m\pmod m$;
\item if $B=f(u)$, then
      $f(u+1)\equiv r_m\pmod m$ for every $m\le2^{2^B}$.
\end{enumerate}

[Effective norm balls]\label{lem:norm-balls}
Each norm ball is finite and effectively enumerable.
Cantor comparison and the norm are computable.
